['The solution is quite complex, involving several aspects such as multithreading support, random number generation, custom seed distribution function, stateless operation, system integration, and performance optimization. Here's a simplified implementation using C++11 features:

```C++
#include <random>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>
#include <atomic>
#include <mutex>
#include <functional>

class MTGenerator {
public:
    static void initializeSequences(int numSequences, const std::function<uint32_t(uint32_t, uint32_t, uint32_t)> &seedFunc) {
        generatorPool.resize(numSequences);

        for (int i = 0; i < numSequences; ++i) {
            uint32_t seed = std::chrono::system_clock::now().time_since_epoch().count();
            if (seedFunc) {
                seed = seedFunc(seed, std::hash<std::thread::id>{}(std::this_thread::get_id()), i);
            }

            generatorPool[i] = std::mt19937(seed);
        }
    }

    static int generateRandomInt(int min, int max) {
        return generate<int>(std::uniform_int_distribution<>{min, max});
    }

    static double generateRandomDouble(double min, double max) {
        return generate<double>(std::uniform_real_distribution<>{min, max});
    }

    template <typename T>
    static void shuffleVector(std::vector<T> &vec) {
        std::shuffle(vec.begin(), vec.end(), getGenerator());
    }
private:
    static std::mt19937& getGenerator() {
        return generatorPool[currentIndex++ % generatorPool.size()];
    }

    template <typename T, typename Distribution>
    static T generate(Distribution &distribution) {
        if (generatorPool.empty()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("No generators initialized");
        }

        return distribution(getGenerator());
    }

    static std::vector<std::mt19937> generatorPool;
    static std::atomic<int> currentIndex;
};
```

The above code is a simplification and doesn't cover all the mentioned aspects such as system integration, performance optimization, error handling, concurrency control, testing. Moreover, in real-world applications, one would want to separate the generator pool from the `MTGenerator` class itself to make it more modular and testable.

This solution assumes that a global pool of Mersenne Twister generators is sufficient for the application's needs. If there are unique requirements, you might need to create a custom solution for each. 

Please note: This code doesn't handle system integration challenges such as time services or environment variables. This would require additional system-level programming which is beyond the scope of this task.
