In this Java Spring Boot app, we have a `DataService` that's responsible for all the business logic. It interacts with different external APIs and a cloud-based storage service to ingest and process data streams. The caching mechanism is handled by a separate `CacheManager` class which stores theme resources and invalidates them as necessary.

```java
@Service
public class DataService {
    private final ConcurrentMap<String, FluxSink<Data>> subscriptions = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void subscribe(ExternalApi api) {
        // Logic to connect with external API and start receiving data 

        Flux.create(sink -> this.subscriptions.put(api.getId(), sink), FluxSink.OverflowStrategy.LATEST)
            .doOnNext(data -> saveToStorageAndValidate(data)) // Save data and handle potential errors 
            .onBackpressureDrop() // Drop items if the downstream can't keep up
            .subscribe();
    }

    public void pauseStreaming() {
        this.subscriptions.values().forEach(FluxSink::complete);
    }

    public void resumeStreaming() {
        // Logic to reconnect and continue receiving data 

        Flux.create(sink -> this.subscriptions.put(api.getId(), sink), FluxSinks.OverflowStrategy.LATEST)
            .doOnNext(data -> saveToStorageAndValidate(data)) // Save data and handle potential errors 
            .onBackpressureDrop() // Drop items if the downstream can't keep up
            .subscribe();
    }
}
```
Unit tests are also necessary to cover all the critical paths in your service. For example, you could test your `resumeStreaming` method after calling `pauseStreaming`. 

Please note that this is a simplified version of what a real-world application would look like and it might require more complex handling for different APIs, error recovery mechanisms, metrics collection etc. Also, Spring's WebFlux framework provides reactive programming support which can help in handling high-volume data streams efficiently.
