['Given the complexity of the task, it's not feasible to provide a complete solution within this context, but here is a simplified example that illustrates how you might approach the problem. 

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.HttpURLConnection;
import java.net.URL;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import org.apache.commons.io.IOUtils;

public class DeserializationService {
    private ObjectMapper objectMapper = new ObjectMapper();
    private HttpURLConnection apiConnection;

    // Cache to reduce redundant API calls
    private Map<String, String> metadataCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public List<CustomObject> deserialize(byte[] data) {
        if (data == null || data.length == 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Data cannot be empty or null");
        }

        List<CustomObject> objects = new ArrayList<>();

        try (ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(data); 
             ObjectInputStream in = new CustomObjectInputStream(bis)) {

            while (true) {
                CustomObject obj = (CustomObject) in.readObject();

                // Fetch and cache metadata for each object
                String metadata = fetchMetadata(obj);
                if (metadata != null) {
                    objects.add(obj);
                }
            }
        } catch (EOFException e) {
            // End of data reached, no more objects to read
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("Deserialization failure: " + e.getMessage());
        } 

        return objects;
    }

    private String fetchMetadata(CustomObject obj) throws IOException {
        String metadata = metadataCache.get(obj);
        if (metadata == null) {
            URL url = new URL("http://api.example.com/objects/" + obj.getId() + "/metadata");
            HttpURLConnection connection = (HttpURLConnection) url.openConnection();

            int responseCode = connection.getResponseCode();
            if (responseCode == HttpURLConnection.HTTP_OK) {
                metadata = IOUtils.toString(connection.getInputStream());

                // Cache the fetched metadata
                metadataCache.putIfAbsent(obj, metadata);
            } else {
                System.out.println("Failed to fetch metadata for object " + obj.getId() + ", response code: " + responseCode);
            }
        }

        return metadata;
    }

    class CustomObjectInputStream extends ObjectInputStream {
        // Accepts only whitelisted classes
        private static final List<String> WHITELISTED_CLASSES = Arrays.asList("com.example.CustomObject", "java.util.ArrayList");

        public CustomObjectInputStream(InputStream in) throws IOException {
            super(in);
        }

        @Override
        protected Class<?> resolveClass(ObjectStreamClass desc) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
            if (WHITELISTED_CLASSES.contains(desc.getName())) {
                return super.resolveClass(desc);
            } else {
                throw new StreamCorruptedException("Unexpected class during deserialization: " + desc.getName());
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In the code above, `CustomObject` is a placeholder for the classes you expect to deserialize from the byte array, and `"com.example.CustomObject", "java.util.ArrayList"` should be replaced with your actual class names. The service will only deserialize objects of these types, preventing potential security issues or excessive garbage collection from untrusted input.

The `fetchMetadata()` method demonstrates how you could use an external API to fetch metadata for each object, caching the results in a concurrent map to reduce redundant calls when possible. The API URL should be replaced with your actual API's endpoint. 

Please note that this is a simplified example and doesn't include all the requirements detailed in the problem (such as metrics logging or error handling for network failures). It also lacks unit tests, proper exception handling, and code organization practices. For a full solution, you would likely need to refactor and add much more complex code.
']

