["Here is a high-level design of an asynchronous file upload service in JavaScript using `axios`, `bluebird` for Promise management and `fs` module for reading files. Note that this code snippet only provides the skeleton and does not include full implementation. Full implementation would require additional services like cloud storage SDKs, caching mechanisms etc.

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');
const bluebird = require('bluebird');
const fs = require('fs');
const crypto = require('crypto');

class UploadService {
    constructor(config) {
        this.uploadUrl = config.uploadUrl; // cloud storage provider URL
        this.tokenManager = config.tokenManager; // token management service
        this.logger = config.logger; // logging service
    }

    async uploadFile(filePath, metadata) {
        const fileStream = fs.createReadStream(filePath);
        const checksum = crypto.createHash('md5');

        fileStream.on('data', function(chunk) {
            checksum.update(chunk);
        });

        await new bluebird((resolve, reject) => {
            fileStream.once('end', () => {
                const hash = checksum.digest('hex');

                this.tokenManager.getToken()
                    .then(token => {
                        axios({
                            method: 'post',
                            url: this.uploadUrl,
                            data: fileStream,
                            headers: {
                                'Authorization': `Bearer ${token}`,
                                'Content-MD5': hash,
                                // other necessary headers
                            },
                        })
                        .then(response => resolve(response))
                        .catch(error => reject(error));
                    });
            });
        });
    }

    // additional methods for input validation, error handling etc. can be added here 
}
```
The above class encapsulates a file upload operation in an asynchronous manner using JavaScript's built-in Promises and async/await syntax. It uses `axios` for making the HTTP request to cloud storage provider and `fs` module for reading files, calculating checksums and streaming data. The token management is delegated to another service (not shown here) which handles OAuth2 token lifecycle including caching etc. 

This class also integrates with a logging mechanism (again not implemented here), allowing for detailed logging of upload progress and failures. 

To adapt this code to any cloud storage provider, you'd need to replace the URL and headers in `axios` call. Additionally, it would be necessary to implement various other features such as input validation, error handling, performance optimization etc.
"]

