['Creating such an application requires extensive design considerations, code structure, and several complex libraries and frameworks. The task you're asking is very high level and can be simplified into many specific tasks which can be completed one by one. However, here's a simple example of how you might setup a thread to handle fetching data from an API:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <curl/curl.h>  // Make sure you have libcurl installed and this is the correct include path for it

// Thread function that fetches data from a REST API endpoint
void fetch_data(std::string url) {
    CURL *curl;
    CURLcode res;

    curl = curl_easy_init();
    if(curl) {
        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, url.c_str());

        // Perform the request
        res = curl_easy_perform(curl);

        // Check for errors
        if(res != CURLE_OK) {
            fprintf(stderr, "curl_easy_perform() failed: %s\n", curl_easy_strerror(res));
        }

        /* always cleanup */ 
        curl_easy_cleanup(curl);
    }
}

int main() {
    // List of REST API endpoints to fetch data from
    std::vector<std::string> urls = {"http://api1.com/data", "http://api2.com/data"};

    // Create a thread for each endpoint
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    for(auto &url : urls) {
        threads.push_back(std::thread(fetch_data, url));
    }

    // Wait for all threads to finish
    for(auto &th : threads) {
        th.join();
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This is just a starting point and does not address many of the additional requirements you've listed such as advanced error handling, configuration management, data validation or logging. Each of these can be expanded upon, but it would require hundreds or even thousands of lines of code to achieve all the features you requested in a single application. 

