3. Data Processing Layer - This is where you would validate data, filter it into acceptable values or discard those that are outside the valid range, and perform other necessary computations on the data.
4. Concurrency/Multi-threaded Layer - This layer uses threads (or possibly multiple processes for better isolation) to handle different API endpoints.
5. Metrics & Monitoring - For this you could look into using something like Prometheus or Grafana, which are designed to provide real-time visibility into what's happening in your application.
6. Error Handling/Fallback Strategies - This would handle various types of errors and failures gracefully (such as network outages, API unavailability, etc). 
7. Security Layer - For this you could look into libraries such as libtls or OpenSSL to help with HTTPS communication.
8. Test Suite - You might create a separate test suite using a library like GoogleTest. This would simulate real-world scenarios and ensure your application behaves correctly under these conditions. 

Remember, this is only one approach to solving the problem, and there may be many others equally valid. The complexity of the solution will also depend heavily on how you want to deploy and manage your application. A containerized solution like Docker might be a good choice for easy deployment and scaling, while a more traditional installation with init scripts would work as well.
