['Here's a basic implementation of such a function in JavaScript using Promises and Async/Await to manage asynchronous operations. This example uses streams from NodeJS but can be adapted for any other libraries or systems that provide similar functionality. 

Please note, this is a very simplified approach and lacks many features requested (like configurable buffer sizes and character encodings, error handling strategies, high throughput scenarios support etc.) which require more complex solution involving streams management, buffering, backpressure control, data validation and other sophisticated techniques.

```js
const { Readable } = require('stream');
const fetch = require('node-fetch'); // use axios or any other similar library for making http requests

class ConcatenateStreams {
  constructor(logger) {
    this.logger = logger;
    this.outputStream = new Readable({ objectMode: true });
    this._arrPromises = [];
  }

  async addStreamFromUrl(url, options = {}) {
    const promise = fetch(url, options)
      .then((response) => {
        if (!response.ok) throw new Error('HTTP error ' + response.status);
        return response;
      })
      .catch((error) => { 
        this.logger.error("Error fetching data from URL", url, error);
        throw error; // rethrow error to be handled in the final catch block
      });
    this._arrPromises.push(promise);
  }

  async getOutput() {
    try {
      let results = await Promise.all(this._arrPromises);

      for (let res of results) {
        const textData = await res.text(); // this could be replaced by any appropriate data transformation based on the actual requirements and the kind of streams we are dealing with 
        this.outputStream.push(textData);
      }

      this.outputStream.push(null); // end stream
    } catch (error) {
      this.logger.error("Error in fetching or processing data", error);
      throw error;
    } finally {
      this._arrPromises = [];
    }

    return this.outputStream;
  }
}
```

This class `ConcatenateStreams` manages multiple streams and provides a single output stream that emits data from all of them. It uses the fetch API to make HTTP requests, but can be adapted for any other external dependencies or third-party APIs. The class is designed with testability in mind and separates concerns into clearly defined methods.
']

