For simplicity, we will use an example with addition only as our Expression Language. This language consists only of pairs of integers separated by "+". We'll generate LLVM IR for that.

```rust
extern crate lexer;
extern crate parser;
extern crate llvm_ir;

use lexer::lex;
use parser::parse;
use llvm_ir::generate;
use std::fs;

pub fn parse_file(filename: String) -> Result<String, String> {
    // Read file content into a string.
    let content = match fs::read_to_string(filename) {
        Ok(content) => content,
        Err(_error) => return Err("Error reading file.".to_string()),
    };

    // Lexing: Transform the content into tokens.
    let tokens = lex(&content);

    println debug "Tokens: {:?}", tokens;

    // Parsing: Transform tokens into an AST (Abstract Syntax Tree).
    let ast = match parse(tokens) {
        Ok(ast) => ast,
        Err(_error) => return Err("Error parsing file.".to_string()),
    };

    println debug "AST: {:?}", ast;

    // Generate LLVM IR code from the AST.
    let llvm_ir = generate(&ast);

    println debug "LLVM IR: {}", llvm_ir;

    Ok(llvm_ir)
}
```

Remember, in a real-world scenario you would need to handle file reading errors and lexer/parser errors properly. 

