['Here is the PHP function for adding random vertices and edges to a graph database. Note that you'll need an existing connection to a Graph Database in order to use it. For simplicity, I am assuming Neo4j here but it can be adapted for any other graph databases as well.

```php
<?php
use GraphAware\Neo4j\Client\ClientBuilder;
require_once 'vendor/autoload.php';

function addRandomVerticesAndEdges($client) {
    // Random number of vertices to create
    $numVertices = rand(1, 10);

    // Create the vertices
    for ($i = 0; $i < $numVertices; $i++) {
        $vertexId = $this->createVertex($client);

        // For each vertex created, create a random number of edges to other nodes.
        $numEdges = rand(1, $numVertices - 1);

        for ($j = 0; $j < $numEdges; $j++) {
            $this->createEdgeToRandomVertex($client, $vertexId);
        }
    }
}

function createVertex($client) {
    $query = 'CREATE (n:Node) RETURN id(n)';
    $result = $client->run($query);

    return $result->firstRecord()->get('id(n)');
}

function createEdgeToRandomVertex($client, $fromId) {
    $toId = rand(1, 99999); // Assuming a maximum of 99,999 vertices exist. Adjust as necessary.

    // You may want to add additional logic here to ensure the "to" vertex exists before creating an edge.
    // If not, you'll need to handle the exception and retry or notify the user accordingly.

    $query = 'MATCH (a:Node), (b:Node) WHERE id(a) = $fromId AND id(b) = $toId CREATE (a)-[:LINKED_TO]->(b)';
    $client->run($query, ['fromId' => $fromId, 'toId' => $toId]);
}
?>
``` 
This is a basic example and you may need to adjust it according to your needs. For instance, you might want to handle exceptions more gracefully or add additional logic for edge creation. Also, the random vertex id generation assumes that vertices are created sequentially with no gaps in their ids, which may not be true if deletions have occurred recently.
