However, I can provide an example of how you might start to build such a system in C:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    int connectionId;
    time_t lastActiveTimestamp;
    bool isActive;
} NetworkConnectionStatus;

typedef struct Node {
    NetworkConnectionStatus data;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;  // This would be replaced by a more complex data structure in a real-world scenario
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void addConnection(NetworkConnectionStatus status) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    newNode->data = status;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void removeConnection(int connectionId) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (head == NULL) {
        return;
    }

    Node* current = head;
    Node* prev = NULL;

    while (current != NULL && current->data.connectionId != connectionId) {
        prev = current;
        current = current->next;
    }

    if (current == NULL) {  // The element was not found in the list
        return;
    }

    if (prev == NULL) {  // We need to remove the head of the list
        head = current->next;
    } else {
        prev->next = current->next;
    }

    free(current);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This is a basic implementation that handles concurrent access and thread safety using a mutex. The rest of the library would need to be implemented with similar care regarding concurrency and correctness. 

However, it's important to stress that building such a system from scratch would likely be complex and time-consuming without a clear design document or specification. It's recommended to use existing libraries or frameworks for this kind of functionality where possible. If you need to implement your own library, consider using a language like C++ that supports more high-level abstractions than C does.
