['Creating such a comprehensive system would require considerable time and resources. However, I can provide you with a simplified example of a C library that manages connections using semaphores:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

// We will use a simple struct to represent our network connection status.
typedef struct {
    int conn_id;
    long last_active_ts;
    int active_status;
} NetworkConnectionStatus;

sem_t semaphore;
NetworkConnectionStatus *conn = NULL;
int size = 0;
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Initialize the library with a specified initial value for the semaphore.
void initialize(int initVal) {
    if (sem_init(&semaphore, 0, initVal)) {
        perror("Failed to initialize semaphore");
    }
}

// Add a connection to the pool.
void addConnection(NetworkConnectionStatus *newConn) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    // Lock and increase semaphore, wait until operation is done before continuing.
    if (sem_wait(&semaphore)) {
        perror("Failed to lock semaphore");
    } else {
        size++;
        conn = realloc(conn, sizeof(*conn) * size);
        memcpy(&conn[size - 1], newConn, sizeof(*newConn));
    }

    // Unlock the semaphore after operation is done.
    if (sem_post(&semaphore)) {
        perror("Failed to unlock semaphore");
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

// Remove a connection from the pool.
void removeConnection(int id) {
    // Lock and decrease semaphore, wait until operation is done before continuing.
    if (sem_wait(&semaphore)) {
        perror("Failed to lock semaphore");
    } else {
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            if (conn[i].conn_id == id) {
                conn[i] = conn[size - 1];
                size--;
                conn = realloc(conn, sizeof(*conn) * size);
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    // Unlock the semaphore after operation is done.
    if (sem_post(&semaphore)) {
        perror("Failed to unlock semaphore");
    }
}
```

This code creates a simple library that manages a pool of `NetworkConnectionStatus` structures, each including a connection ID, last active timestamp, and an active status flag. It uses a mutex lock and a semaphore for synchronization, ensuring thread-safe concurrent access in a high-traffic server environment.

Remember to build your code incrementally with tests along the way to ensure that you haven't broken anything when making changes. Remember to always check return values from library functions like `sem_wait` and `sem_post` to catch errors as close to where they occur as possible, rather than at some point farther down the call stack.
']

