['The following is a high level overview of how you could approach this task:

1. First, define your message structure using a schema language like Apache Avro or Protocol Buffers (protobuf). This way, the format can be easily updated without breaking changes to any existing consumers.

2. Next, design your messaging system as a set of producer/consumer microservices communicating via message queues. This decouples services from each other and allows them to scale independently. You could use an open-source tool like Apache Kafka for this purpose. 

3. To ensure exactly-once semantics, you can implement idempotent consumers that consume messages only once even if they crash or retry.

4. For backpressure and message retention, consider using a time-based expiry policy with an external database to store the messages. Use a dead-letter queue to hold failed messages for further analysis. 

5. To handle concurrency effectively, use distributed locks and transaction management tools. This will ensure that only one consumer processes each message at any given time.

6. Implement fault tolerance by having multiple replicas of your services running in different nodes and using a load balancer to distribute incoming requests evenly across them. If one node fails, the others can continue serving traffic.

7. To optimize performance, use asynchronous programming techniques and batch processing whenever possible. Use an efficient data structure for storing messages and make sure you are releasing resources properly when they are no longer needed. 

8. Regularly profile and test your system under different load conditions to identify any potential bottlenecks. This will help you optimize the system for peak usage periods.

Please note that this is a high-level overview of how you could approach this task, and each step would require a fair amount of detailed design and implementation work. You'll need to handle security, error handling, logging, monitoring, etc., as well. 

Here's a very basic example in Java using RabbitMQ:

```java
import com.rabbitmq.client.*;

public class MessageSender {
    private final static String QUEUE_NAME = "hello";

    public static void main(String[] argv) throws Exception {
        ConnectionFactory factory = new ConnectionFactory();
        factory.setHost("localhost");
        try (Connection connection = factory.newConnection();
             Channel channel = connection.createChannel()) {
            channel.queueDeclare(QUEUE_NAME, false, false, false, null);
            String message = "Hello World!";
            channel.basicPublish("", QUEUE_NAME, null, message.getBytes());
            System.out.println(" [x] Sent '" + message + "'");
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a connection to a RabbitMQ server and sends a message to a queue named "hello". 

For the consumer side, you would need something like this:

```java
import com.rabbitmq.client.*;

public class MessageReceiver {
    private final static String QUEUE_NAME = "hello";

    public static void main(String[] argv) throws Exception {
        ConnectionFactory factory = new ConnectionFactory();
        factory.setHost("localhost");
        Connection connection = factory.newConnection();
        Channel channel = connection.createChannel();

        channel.queueDeclare(QUEUE_NAME, false, false, false, null);
        System.out.println(" [*] Waiting for messages. To exit press CTRL+C");

        DeliverCallback deliverCallback = (consumerTag, delivery) -> {
            String message = new String(delivery.getBody(), "UTF-8");
            System.out.println(" [x] Received '" + message + "'");
        };
        channel.basicConsume(QUEUE_NAME, true, deliverCallback, consumerTag -> { });
    }
}
```
This code creates a connection to the same RabbitMQ server and waits for messages in the "hello" queue. When it receives a message, it prints out its content. 

Please be aware that this is just a simple example. A production-ready solution would involve more complexity and considerations such as error handling, scaling, fault tolerance, etc.
']

