["Here is a basic implementation of an Octree data structure in C++ which can be extended to integrate with an external database and handle concurrent access using locks, but implementing all the detailed requirements mentioned would result in a huge codebase.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <array>
#include <mutex>

class OctreeNode {
public:
    std::array<OctreeNode*, 8> children;
    std::vector<float*> dataPoints; // points are assumed to be float[3] arrays.
    std::recursive_mutex mutex;

    void insert(const float point[]) {
        int index = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i)
            if (!children[i]->insert(point, index)) 
                return;

        std::lock_guard<std::recursive_mutex> lock(mutex);
        dataPoints.push_back((float*) point); // const-cast, assuming the caller won't modify 'point'.
    }

private:
    bool insert(const float point[], int& index) {
        if (index < 3 && point[index] < children[0]->boundingBox[1][index]) // bounding box check. 
            return children[0]->insert(point, ++index);

        for (int i = 4; i < 8; ++i)
            if (children[i]->boundingBox[0][index] <= point[index] && point[index] < children[i]->boundingBox[1][index]) 
                return children[i]->insert(point, ++index);

        return false; // Point is outside the bounding box.
    }
};
```

Please note that this code does not include the full features of a concurrent and multithreaded system with an external database or real-time operations handling. It's only an overview of how such an octree-based spatial partitioning system might be implemented in C++. For all these details, you will need to use specific libraries/frameworks that support multi-threading (e.g., Boost Asio for networking), concurrency control mechanisms (std::mutex, std::lock_guard), etc., and integrate it with an external database system (MySQL, PostgreSQL, etc.) through suitable APIs or ORMs like SQLAlchemy for Python, Hibernate for Java, etc.
