Writing all the mentioned features in C from scratch may take a lot of time and effort which would make the post very large and less readable. It's also important to remember that the code for these features can vary greatly depending on the specific requirements of your project.

As such, I will provide you with a simple example of how you could start implementing parts of this service in C, including DNS resolution, caching and rate limiting mechanisms:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <arpa/inet.h> // for inet_pton() function
#include <time.h>      // for time() function

// For simplicity's sake, let's assume a very basic in-memory cache
struct DNSCacheEntry {
    char ip[INET6_ADDRSTRLEN];  // max length of IPv6 address string
    time_t timestamp;           // timestamp to track when entry was added
};
#define CACHE_SIZE 1024         // simple cache size constant
struct DNSCacheEntry dnsCache[CACHE_SIZE] = {0};

// Assume we have a function that fetches IP from DNS server
const char* fetchIPFromDNS(char *hostname) {}

// Function to resolve DNS and apply rate limiting
void resolveDNSWithRateLimit(char *clientID, char *hostname, int maxQueriesPerMinute, int totalIPsResolved) {
    static int queriesThisMinute = 0;  // for rate limiting
    static int totalIPsResolvedAllClients = 0;  // for rate limiting
    time_t currentTime = time(NULL);

    // Check cache first to see if we have the IP address already
    for (int i = 0; i < CACHE_SIZE; ++i) {
        if (strcmp(hostname, dnsCache[i].ip) == 0) {
            printf("IP Address: %s\n", dnsCache[i].ip);
            return;
        }
    }

    // Check rate limiting before making DNS request
    if (queriesThisMinute >= maxQueriesPerMinute || totalIPsResolvedAllClients >= totalIPsResolved) {
        printf("Rate limit exceeded\n");
        return;
    }

    const char *ip = fetchIPFromDNS(hostname);  // assuming this function exists and returns an IP address as string

    // Add entry to cache
    for (int i = 0; i < CACHE_SIZE; ++i) {
        if (!dnsCache[i].timestamp || dnsCache[i].timestamp < currentTime - 60 * 5) {  // entries older than 5 minutes are replaced
            strncpy(dnsCache[i].ip, ip, INET6_ADDRSTRLEN);
            dnsCache[i].timestamp = currentTime;
            ++queriesThisMinute;
            ++totalIPsResolvedAllClients;
            printf("IP Address: %s\n", ip);
            return;
        }
    }

    // If cache is full, replace the oldest entry
    strncpy(dnsCache[0].ip, ip, INET6_ADDRSTRLEN);
    dnsCache[0].timestamp = currentTime;
    ++queriesThisMinute;
    ++totalIPsResolvedAllClients;
    printf("IP Address: %s\n", ip);
}
```

However, this is a simplification and may not fully meet your project's requirements. It is recommended to approach this in stages, starting with simpler parts of the system, like DNS resolution and caching, and gradually building up to the full service. You would also likely want to use an existing networking library for better performance and robustness, rather than implementing a custom one.
']

