["Here is a basic implementation of what you asked for in C using pthread library to manage semaphores. It's not a full-fledged solution but it should give you an idea on how to start implementing such a system. 

```C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

// Define semaphore with dynamic initial value and support for dynamic adjustment
typedef struct {
    int value;
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
    pthread_cond_t cond;
} Semaphore;

void initSemaphore(Semaphore *s, int value) {
    s->value = value;
    pthread_mutex_init(&(s->mutex), NULL);
    pthread_cond_init(&(s->cond), NULL);
}

void P(Semaphore *s) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&(s->mutex));
    while (s->value <= 0) {
        pthread_cond_wait(&(s->cond), &(s->mutex));
    }
    s->value--;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&(s->mutex));
}

void V(Semaphore *s) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&(s->mutex));
    ++s->value;
    pthread_cond_signal(&(s->cond));
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&(s->mutex));
}
```
This is a basic implementation of the semaphore management system. It includes mechanisms for initialization, P and V operations, and thread safety. 

Please note that real-world implementations would likely involve more considerations such as error handling, logging integration, timeout mechanism implementation, performance monitoring, scalability optimizations and testing. You may also want to consider using a proven library or framework for managing semaphores, instead of rolling your own. It's critical to select components based on their robustness and community support rather than re-inventing the wheel. 

This code does not include any logging mechanism or timeout handling due to simplicity and complexity concerns, but you could easily add them with other pthread synchronization primitives like `pthread_cond_timedwait()`. Similarly, for comprehensive error handling and recovery strategies, you would likely want to use exception handling mechanisms in C++ instead of raw POSIX API calls.
"]

