['Here is a very simplified code snippet that shows the general structure of the class `MoSTStateLoader`:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.lang.*;
import com.amazonaws.services.s3.*;
import org.postgresql.jdbc4.*;

public class MoSTStateLoader {
    private static final int MAX_CACHE_SIZE = 1024 * 1024; // 1MB

    private DiskStorage diskStorage = new DiskStorage();
    private CloudObjectStorage cloudStorage = new CloudObjectStorage(new AmazonS3());
    private RelationalDatabase relationalDB = new RelationalDatabase(DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:postgresql://localhost/test"));

    private Map<String, MoSTState> cache = Collections.synchronizedMap(new LinkedHashMap<>(MAX_CACHE_SIZE, 0.75f, true) {
        protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry eldest) {
            return size() > MAX_CACHE_SIZE;
        }
    });

    public MoSTState loadSync(String key) throws IOException {
        if (cache.containsKey(key)) {
            return cache.get(key);
        }

        MoSTState state = diskStorage.loadFromDisk(key);
        if (state == null) {
            state = cloudStorage.loadFromCloud(key);
        }
        if (state == null) {
            state = relationalDB.loadFromDB(key);
        }

        cache.put(key, state);
        return state;
    }

    public CompletableFuture<MoSTState> loadAsync(String key) throws IOException {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> loadSync(key));
    }

    // ... similar methods for diskStorage, cloudStorage and relationalDB
}
``` 
Please note that this is a simplified code snippet and doesn't contain the actual implementation of DiskStorage, CloudObjectStorage, RelationalDatabase or any other classes it uses. Also, handling exceptions in real-world scenarios will be much more sophisticated than what you see here, as well as ensuring secure authentication with external systems, managing quotas for S3, and so on.

Additionally, this code only demonstrates a basic caching mechanism which might not suit all use cases. Depending upon the specific requirements, other eviction policies like LRU or LFU could be better suited. 

Finally, this doesn't include any of the complexities related to serialization formats, versioning, security and monitoring. The code would be considerably larger with these features included.
