['The given task is very extensive and would require a substantial codebase to be written. However, here's an attempt at creating a basic structure for the system you described using Rust. Please note that this is a simplified example and in reality, you'd need to handle many other edge cases, integrate with real third-party APIs etc.

```rust
use std::collections::VecDeque;
use chrono::prelude::*;
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;
use serde_json::Value;
use anyhow::Result;

#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
struct Transaction {
    amount: f64,
    timestamp: DateTime<Utc>,
}

impl Transaction {
    fn new(amount: f64) -> Self {
        Self {
            amount,
            timestamp: Utc::now(),
        }
    }
}

struct TransactionProcessor {
    transactions: VecDeque<Transaction>,
    total_sum: f64,
}

impl TransactionProcessor {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            transactions: VecDeque::new(),
            total_sum: 0.0,
        }
    }

    fn add(&mut self, transaction: Transaction) {
        if self.transactions.len() >= 15 {
            let popped = self.transactions.pop_front().unwrap();
            self.total_sum -= popped.amount;
        }

        self.transactions.push_back(transaction);
        self.total_sum += transaction.amount;
    }

    fn moving_average(&self) -> f64 {
        if self.transactions.is_empty() {
            return 0.0;
        }

        self.total_sum / (self.transactions.len() as f64)
    }
}

fn main() -> Result<()> {
    let mut processor = TransactionProcessor::new();

    loop {
        // Simulate incoming transactions
        for _ in 0..5 {
            let transaction_amount = 10.0;
            processor.add(Transaction::new(transaction_amount));

            println_str(&format_str("Added transaction of {}", &transaction_amount)?);
            thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(5));
        }

        let moving_average = processor.moving_average();
        println_str(&format_str("Moving Average: ", &moving_average)?;
    }
}
```
This is a simplistic approach and does not handle many factors like network requests, database management etc. You'd need to use async programming, web framework (like Actix or Rocket), an ORM library for database operations, and a logging solution to get the full feature system. This also needs proper error handling which is omitted in this basic example.
']

