To implement the disjoint pairs check in Java, we would need to use a DisjointSet data structure:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    static int find(int node, int parent[]) {
        if (parent[node] == -1)
            return node;
        return find(parent[node], parent);
    }

    static void union_set(int ancestor_x, int ancestor_y, int parent[], int rank[]) {
        if (rank[ancestor_x] < rank[ancestor_y])
            parent[ancestor_x] = ancestor_y;
        else if (rank[ancestor_y] < rank[ancestor_x])
            parent[ancestor_y] = ancestor_x;
        else {
            parent[ancestor_x] = ancestor_y;
            rank[ancestor_y]++;
        }
    }

    static int countDisjointPairs(int arr[]) {
        int n = arr.length;
        int parent[] = new int[100];
        int rank[] = new int[100];

        Arrays.fill(parent, -1);
        Arrays.fill(rank, 0);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int ancestor_i = find(arr[i], parent);

            if (ancestor_i != arr[i])
                union_set(ancestor_i, arr[i], parent, rank);
        }

        HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int ancestor_i = find(arr[i], parent);

            if (!set.contains(ancestor_i))
                set.add(ancestor_i);
        }

        return set.size();
    }

    public static void main (String[] args) {
        int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        System.out.println("Number of disjoint pairs: " + countDisjointPairs(arr));
    }
}
```
Please note that this is a high-level overview and does not contain all the details required for a production system such as exception handling, error logging, and much more. Each component requires extensive design, implementation and testing.
