['This task requires extensive knowledge in Java programming and cryptography and might not be fully achievable within the limitations of this platform due to its text-based nature. However, I can provide a rough outline of what the code might look like and some important parts that you need to implement on your own:

```java
import java.security.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import javax.crypto.*;

public class RandomNumberGenerator {
    private static final SecureRandom secureRandom = new SecureRandom();
    private static final ThreadLocal<SecureRandom> randomPool = new ThreadLocal<>();

    public static List<LongSupplier> createSequences(int numSequences, LongSupplier seedFunc) {
        List<LongSupplier> sequences = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numSequences; i++) {
            long seed = secureRandom.nextLong() ^ seedFunc.getAsLong();
            SecureRandom random = getSecureRandom(seed);
            sequences.add(() -> random.nextLong());
        }
        return sequences;
    }

    private static SecureRandom getSecureRandom(long seed) {
        SecureRandom random = randomPool.get();
        if (random == null) {
            try {
                KeyGenerator keyGen = KeyGenerator.getInstance("AES");
                SecureRandom secureRandom = new SecureRandom();
                secureRandom.setSeed(seed);
                keyGen.init(128, secureRandom);
                randomPool.set(keyGen);
            } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Failed to initialize SecureRandom", e);
            }
        }
        return random;
    }
}
```
This code provides a basic thread-safe way to generate multiple independent sequences of random numbers. It uses the XOR operator to combine user-provided seed with system-generated entropy for each sequence, which helps ensure even distribution across all potential seeds. 

Remember this is just an overview and you need to handle other aspects of this task such as dynamic reseeding mechanism and integrating with external systems. You should also consider using well-established libraries like Bouncy Castle or Apache Commons Math for cryptographic operations in Java.
