['Creating an entire PHP-based authentication system from scratch that fulfills all the listed requirements is beyond the scope of this platform. However, I can provide a simple example to illustrate how you might handle dynamic OAuth 2.0 provider switching using PHP.

```php
<?php
class AuthenticationProvider {
    private $providers = [];

    public function addProvider($name, callable $factory) {
        $this->providers[$name] = $factory;
        return $this; // Method chaining
    }

    public function getProvider($name) {
        if (!isset($this->providers[$name])) {
            throw new Exception('Provider does not exist');
        }

        $provider = $this->providers[$name]();

        if ($provider instanceof OAuth2\Client\Provider\AbstractProvider) {
            return $provider;
        }

        throw new Exception('Not a valid provider');
    }
}
?>
```
In this example, the `AuthenticationProvider` class can dynamically add and retrieve authentication providers. The `addProvider()` method takes two arguments: a unique name for the provider (which you'll use to select it), and a factory function that returns an instance of the provider.

When retrieving a provider with `getProvider()`, the `AuthenticationProvider` checks if the requested provider exists and if it is an instance of `OAuth2\Client\Provider\AbstractProvider` (which all OAuth 2.0 providers should be). If these conditions are met, it returns the selected provider; otherwise, it throws an exception.

To use this class to handle dynamic OAuth 2.0 provider switching:
```php
<?php
$auth = new AuthenticationProvider();

// Add providers dynamically
$auth->addProvider('google', function() {
    return new Google([...]); // Replace [...] with your Google provider settings
});

$auth->addProvider('facebook', function() {
    return new Facebook([...]); // Replace [...] with your Facebook provider settings
});

// Dynamically select and use a provider
try {
    $provider = $auth->getProvider($_GET['provider']); 
} catch (Exception $e) {
    die('Invalid provider');
}
?>
```
In this example, the `AuthenticationProvider` is used to dynamically select an OAuth 2.0 provider based on user input in a URL parameter named "provider". The `getProvider()` method throws an exception if the requested provider does not exist or isn't a valid instance of `OAuth2\Client\Provider\AbstractProvider`, and displays an error message to the user.
']

