['The solution provided below is a simplified example on how to implement such a module with C programming language. The exact implementation will depend heavily on the specific system requirements, hardware architecture, and performance profiles. 

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
    int RNTI;
    int CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;
    int CRCFLAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

#define MAX_ARRAY_SIZE 10000
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T array[MAX_ARRAY_SIZE];
size_t arraySize = 0;

void addElement(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T element) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (arraySize < MAX_ARRAY_SIZE) {
        array[arraySize++] = element;
    } else {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Array is full. Cannot add new elements.\n");
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T getElementAtIndex(size_t index) {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T element;
    if (index >= MAX_ARRAY_SIZE) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Index out of bounds.\n");
        return element; // Returns an empty element in case of error.
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    element = array[index];
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return element;
}

void updateElementAtIndex(size_t index, NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T newValue) {
    if (index >= MAX_ARRAY_SIZE) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Index out of bounds.\n");
        return;
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    array[index] = newValue;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
``` 
This code creates a fixed-size array to hold `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structures. It uses a mutex (pthread lock) for thread safety, which ensures that only one thread can access the shared data at a time. Each function begins with acquiring the lock and ends with releasing it. This prevents potential race conditions between threads from accessing or modifying the array simultaneously. 

Please note:
- The error handling in this example is very basic and does not include all possible error scenarios. In a real-world application, more robust error checking should be implemented to handle unexpected input or other issues.
