["Here's a high-level example of how this class could be implemented in C++ using various features of the language like concurrency support (`std::thread`), lock-free programming techniques, and exception handling. Note that this code is highly simplified and is not intended to run as-is. It should act as a starting point for your own implementation.

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <future>
#include <mutex>
#include <vector>
// Assume ExternalMediaServerAPI is an interface or class definition provided by the external media server vendor 
class StreamingMediaProcessor {
public:
    StreamingMediaProcessor() : thread_pool(std::thread::hardware_concurrency()) {}

    ~StreamingMediaProcessor() {
        stop();
    }

    void start(ExternalMediaServerAPI& server) {
        running.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
        for (size_t i = 0; i < thread_pool.size(); ++i) {
            thread_pool[i] = std::async(std::launch::async, &StreamingMediaProcessor::process, this, server);
        }
    }

    void stop() {
        running.store(false, std::memory_order_release);
        for (auto& future : thread_pool) {
            if (future.valid()) {
                future.wait();
            }
        }
    }

    void process(ExternalMediaServerAPI& server) {
        while (running.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
            std::vector<Stream> streams = server.getStreamsToProcess();
            for (auto &stream : streams) {
                try {
                    stream.process(); // Assumes process is a method on the Stream class that handles media processing
                } catch(const std::exception& e) {
                    logError(e);  // handle error logging
                    continue;
                }
            }
        }
    }

private:
    std::atomic<bool> running = ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(false);
    std::vector<std::future<void>> thread_pool;
};
```

This code utilizes C++'s `async` and `future` features to create a pool of threads, each executing the `process` method. The `start` function starts processing by setting an atomic boolean flag (to ensure visibility between threads) and spawning a thread for each item in `thread_pool`. 

The `stop` function signals all threads to stop by unsetting the atomic flag, then waits for them to finish using `std::future::wait()`. 

Inside `process` method, it retrieves streams from the server and processes them. The exception handling mechanism is rudimentary: any exceptions raised during media processing are caught and logged before moving on to the next stream. 

Note that this is a high-level sketch of how you might design your class. You will need to expand upon this, including designing classes for `Stream` and integrating with an actual external media server API. This code also doesn't handle load balancing between multiple instances of StreamingMediaProcessor or scaling horizontally across multiple machines, but it should give you a starting point. For more detailed code and performance optimizations, consider leveraging Boost libraries like `Boost.Asio` for networking, `Boost.Thread` for thread pooling, and `Boost.Atomic` for atomic operations.
