['The problem you've described involves several complex aspects that would require a detailed exploration of your specific requirements and constraints, as well as careful design and implementation considerations. Here is a simplified example of how you might structure such an application in C, but remember this is a very basic starting point. It does not cover all the points mentioned above:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Define global variables for shared state
#define MAX_CONCURRENT 5
#define MAX_FILESIZE (1024*1024*1024) // 1GB in bytes
int num_files = 0;
char *filenames[MAX_CONCURRENT];
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Function to process a single file
void* processFile(void* filename) {
    char* filepath = (char*)filename;
    FILE *file = fopen(filepath, "r");
    if (!file) {
        printf("Failed to open %s\n", filepath);
        return NULL;
    }

    // Process the file here...

    fclose(file);
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    num_files--;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return NULL;
}

// Function to handle command-line arguments and start processing
int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc <= 1) {
        printf("No files provided\n");
        return 1;
    }

    pthread_t threads[MAX_CONCURRENT];
    for (int i = 0; i < argc - 1 && num_files < MAX_CONCURRENT; ++i) {
        struct stat st;
        if(stat(argv[i+1], &st) != 0 || st.st_size > MAX_FILESIZE) {
            printf("Invalid file: %s\n", argv[i+1]);
            continue;
        }

        pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
        filenames[num_files] = argv[i+1];
        num_files++;
        pthread_create(&threads[i % MAX_CONCURRENT], NULL, processFile, (void*)argv[i+1]);
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < num_files; ++i) {
        pthread_join(threads[i % MAX_CONCURRENT], NULL);
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This example reads files from the command line, processes them concurrently up to a limit and handles invalid file sizes. It doesn't cover logging, integration with other services or performance optimization in detail. Each of these points would require a more complex solution, likely involving additional libraries and tools. 

Please note that C is not typically used for high-level systems programming tasks due to its lack of advanced features such as garbage collection. Depending on your specific needs, you may want to consider using a different language with better support for these kinds of applications, like Python or Go. However, if performance and control over low-level details are essential then C might be the right choice. 

Remember that writing an efficient command line utility requires careful design decisions and deep understanding of how your system works. Always consider trade-offs between throughput, latency, memory usage and complexity.
