["Designing a task queue system from scratch requires extensive knowledge of distributed systems concepts such as messaging queues, load balancing, fault tolerance, data partitioning, and replication. This is quite a complex problem to solve using code directly. However, I will provide a high-level design overview and some pseudo code for each component. 

Here's an example how you could approach it:
```java
public interface TaskQueue {
    void submitTask(Runnable task);
    Runnable getNextTask();
    void markCompleted(Runnable task);
}

public class CloudTaskQueue implements TaskQueue{
    private final BlockingQueue<Runnable> tasks;
    private final AtomicInteger inFlight = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public CloudTaskQueue() {
        tasks = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();
    }

    @Override
    public void submitTask(Runnable task) {
        tasks.add(task);
    }

    @Override
    public Runnable getNextTask() {
        inFlight.incrementAndGet();
        return tasks.poll();
    }

    @Override
    public void markCompleted(Runnable task) {
        inFlight.decrementAndGet();
    }
}
```
This is a simple blocking queue based implementation of TaskQueue, you'll need to use distributed queues for high availability and fault tolerance as described above. 

