['Creating a full-fledged system like this is beyond the scope of what can be achieved in a single code block due to its complexity and extensive nature. However, I can give you a simple example of how you could start setting up a Kafka producer using Java, which could be extended for the other requirements:

```java
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.util.Properties;

public class SimpleProducer {

    private final static String TOPIC = "test-topic";
    private final static String BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS = "localhost:9092,localhost:9093,localhost:9094";

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        runProducer();
    }

    // Method to publish message on Kafka topic
    private static void runProducer() throws InterruptedException {

        final Producer<String, String> producer = createProducer();

        try {
            for (int index = 0; index < 1000; index++) {
                // Creating record with key and value
                final ProducerRecord<String, String> record = new ProducerRecord<>(TOPIC, "key-" + index, "value-" + index);

                // Publish message to Kafka topic
                producer.send(record).get();
            }
        } finally {
            producer.flush();
            producer.close();
        }
    }

    // Method to create a new Kafka Producer
    private static Producer<String, String> createProducer() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.CLIENT_ID_CONFIG, "KafkaExampleProducer");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());

        return new KafkaProducer<>(props);
    }
}
```
This is a simple producer that publishes 1000 messages to a Kafka topic. You would need additional libraries for Avro, Protobuf and cloud-native data lakes integrations. For advanced configurations like schema evolution, you might want to look at Confluent Schema Registry.

