['Sorry but creating this complex system in an example is beyond my capacity right now. However, I can show you a simplified version of inter process communication using semaphores in C which could be extended for this task. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <fcntl.h>    /* For O_* constants */
#include <sys/stat.h> /* For mode constants */
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    sem_t *sem;
    if ((sem = sem_open("/mysemaphore", O_CREAT, 0644, 1)) == SEM_FAILED) {
        perror("Semaphore initialization failed");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Waiting for semaphore\n");
    if(sem_wait(sem) < 0){ /* wait */
        printf("Error on sem_wait.\n");
    }

    printf("Critical section, press enter to release semaphore... ");
    getchar();

    printf("Releasing Semaphore\n");
    if(sem_post(sem) < 0){ /* post */
        printf("Error on sem_post.\n");
    }

    // close named semaphore
    if (sem_close(sem) < 0) {
        perror("Semaphore Close failed");
        return 1;
    }

    // unlink named semaphore
    if (sem_unlink("/mysemaphore") < 0) {
        perror("Semaphore Unlink failed");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Exiting...\n");
    return 0;
}
```
This code creates a named semaphore using `sem_open`, then waits and posts to it using the `sem_wait` and `sem_post` functions. Note that this program doesn't integrate with an external orchestration service for simplicity. For integration, we would need to replace `sem_post()` and `sem_wait()` calls with appropriate function calls to your orchestrator.

Remember to compile the code using -pthread option: `gcc filename.c -o outputname -pthread` 

To run this program, you can use two terminal windows. One window will be running the compiled executable ('./outputname'), and another will be sending signals via a Unix pipe to wake up the sleeping process ('echo > mypipe'). Replace 'mypipe' with actual named pipe created by orchestrator service.
']

