However, here is a very basic start to demonstrate how we can create a modular structure for our library:

```C
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    int data;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T **array;
    size_t capacity;
    size_t count;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
} ArrayManager;

void initArray(ArrayManager *mgr, size_t initialCapacity) {
    mgr->count = 0;
    mgr->capacity = initialCapacity;
    mgr->array = (NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T **)calloc(initialCapacity, sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *));
    pthread_mutex_init(&mgr->lock, NULL);
}

void addToArray(ArrayManager *mgr, NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *item) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mgr->lock);
    if (mgr->count == mgr->capacity) {
        // Double the capacity of our array when full.
        mgr->capacity *= 2;
        mgr->array = realloc(mgr->array, mgr->capacity * sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *));
    }
    mgr->array[mgr->count++] = item;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mgr->lock);
}
```
This simple example demonstrates a thread-safe array manager that can grow dynamically. It uses `pthread` locks to ensure atomicity and safety under contention conditions, but it does not cover integration with external data sources, network latency handling, or comprehensive testing etc. 

It's recommended you approach this task in stages, each stage building on the previous one while also ensuring that the code is well-documented, maintainable and extensible. For example, begin by implementing a basic dynamic array manager which supports insertions and ensures thread safety. After that, expand upon it to include additional features like error handling, logging, integration with databases/APIs and unit tests. 

