['This is a very complex project that cannot be fully implemented in a single code block due to its extensive complexity. This includes considerations for fault tolerance, high throughput, scalability, security, and many more aspects of distributed system design. Here's an example of how you might start implementing a part of the system using C and RabbitMQ for messaging:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <amqp.h>
#include <amqp_tcp_socket.h>

int main() {
    amqp_connection_state_t conn = amqp_new_connection();

    amqp_bytes_t queuename;
    queuename.len = 5;
    queuename.bytes = "queue";

    int status;
    amqp_socket_t *socket = NULL;
    socket = amqp_tcp_socket_new(conn);
    if (!socket) {
        fprintf(stderr, "creating TCP socket");
        return 1;
    }

    status = amqp_login(conn, "/", 0, 131072, 0, AMQP_SASL_METHOD_PLAIN, "guest", "guest");
    if (status < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Logging in");
        return 1;
    }

    amqp_channel_open(conn, 1);
    amqp_rpc_reply_t res = amqp_get_rpc_reply(conn);
    if (res.reply_type != AMQP_RESPONSE_NORMAL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Opening channel");
        return 1;
    }

    amqp_queue_declare(conn, 1, queuename, 0, 0, 0, 0, amqp_empty_table);
    res = amqp_get_rpc_reply(conn);
    if (res.reply_type != AMQP_RESPONSE_NORMAL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Declaring queue");
        return 1;
    }

    amqp_bytes_t message = amqp_cstring_bytes("Hello World!");
    amqp_basic_publish(conn, 1, amqp_empty_bytes, queuename, 0, 0, NULL, message);
    res = amqp_get_rpc_reply(conn);
    if (res.reply_type != AMQP_RESPONSE_NORMAL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Publishing");
        return 1;
    }

    amqp_channel_close(conn, 1, AMQP_REPLY_SUCCESS);
    res = amqp_get_rpc_reply(conn);
    if (res.reply_type != AMQP_RESPONSE_NORMAL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Closing channel");
        return 1;
    }

    amqp_destroy_connection(conn);
    return 0;
}
```

This is a basic example of how to use RabbitMQ for messaging in C. However, you would need a lot more code and complexity to fully implement the entire system outlined above. It's not possible to provide all that code here, but hopefully this gives you an idea of how to start building a distributed messaging system using C.
']

